.TH "SECURE\-TERMINAL\-SIGREPORT" "1" "January 2020" "secure-terminal" "secure-terminal Manual"
.SH "NAME"
\fBsecure\-terminal\-sigreport\fR \- print received signals (a Terminate test target)
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
\fBsecure\-terminal \-\-\fR \fBsecure\-terminal\-sigreport\fR
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
secure\-terminal\-sigreport traps every catchable signal, prints \fBreceived SIG<NAME> (<num>)\fR when one arrives, and keeps running\. It is a diagnostic target for the Terminate button (and the \fB/terminate\fR command): run it as the terminal's program, click Terminate, and watch the \fBSIGTERM\fR land, followed about two seconds later by the uncatchable \fBSIGKILL\fR that ends the process \-\- confirming the whole SIGTERM\-then\-SIGKILL escalation reached the foreground group\.
.P
\fBSIGKILL\fR and \fBSIGSTOP\fR cannot be caught (the kernel never delivers them to a handler), so they are not trapped; a \fBSIGKILL\fR simply makes the process vanish\.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
Exits 0 on \fBSIGQUIT\fR (Ctrl\-\e)\. Otherwise it runs until killed\.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
\fBsecure\-terminal\fR(1)
